 "docs": {
  "en": {
   "title": "US West freezing level: 0 °C from 7,349 ft to 14,436 ft, snow line up to 13,451 ft (8 November)",
   "description": "The 0 °C level decides whether the mountains store snow or shed rain: over the next days our forecast puts it between 7,349 ft (2,240 m) and …",
   "lede": "The 0 °C level decides whether the mountains store snow or shed rain: over the next days our forecast puts it between 7,349 ft (2,240 m) and 14,436 ft (4,400 m) in the US West. On Sunday 8 November the snow line at Eagle Point reaches 13,451 ft (4,100 m) in the warmest hours, about 984 ft (300 m) under that level. Where the snow line sits decides whether a storm fills the snowpack or runs straight into the rivers.",
   "sections": [
    {
     "h": "Will rain reach the snowpack?",
     "p": [
      "Aspen Snowmass holds about 17 in (43 cm) on its upper slopes, and on 10 November and 11 November the snow line sits above mid-mountain: rain on that snow adds water, makes it heavy and can speed up the melt, then it turns icy when it freezes.",
      "Rain-on-snow risk at Taos Ski Valley: about 15 in (39 cm) on the upper slopes and a snow line above mid-mountain on 10 November and 11 November; the snow would get heavy and wet, then icy as it freezes again.",
      "Crested Butte holds about 17 in (42 cm) on its upper slopes, and on 10 November and 11 November the snow line sits above mid-mountain: rain on that snow adds water, makes it heavy and can speed up the melt, then it turns icy when it freezes.",
      "105 mm of precipitation is forecast at Mt. Baker (5,052 ft (1,540 m)); while it falls, the snow line sits near 5,774 ft (1,760 m)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Against past winters",
     "p": [
      "At about 10,315 ft (3,144 m), Aspen Snowmass has 2 in (5 cm) today; a normal 8 November has 9 in (22 cm) there.",
      "Only one winter in ten has more than 21 in (53 cm) on this date.",
      "A week ago the same spot had 0 in (1 cm).",
      "In past winters the best snow at Aspen Snowmass usually came late March / early April.",
      "A slow start is common and says little about January; for now the highest lifts and the runs with snowmaking are the safe choice."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Rain or snow at each resort",
     "p": [
      "Aspen Snowmass (base 8,117 ft (2,474 m), top 12,516 ft (3,815 m)): the snow line rises to about 12,795 ft (3,900 m) on 9 November, above the top, so precipitation would fall as rain or wet snow on the whole mountain and run off.",
      "At Taos Ski Valley (base 9,350 ft (2,850 m), top 11,821 ft (3,603 m)), a snow line of about 13,123 ft (4,000 m) on 8 November sits above the top: rain or wet snow on every slope if precipitation falls.",
      "Crested Butte (base 9,131 ft (2,783 m), top 11,444 ft (3,488 m)): the snow line rises to about 13,123 ft (4,000 m) on 9 November, above the top, so precipitation would fall as rain or wet snow on the whole mountain and run off.",
      "At Beartooth Basin Summer (base 10,377 ft (3,163 m), top 10,922 ft (3,329 m)) the snow line of about 10,171 ft (3,100 m) on 8 November stays below the base: everything that falls is stored as snow.",
      "At Eagle Point (base 9,327 ft (2,843 m), top 10,571 ft (3,222 m)), a snow line of about 13,451 ft (4,100 m) on 8 November sits above the top: rain or wet snow on every slope if precipitation falls."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "The 0 °C level and the melt",
     "p": [
      "The 0 °C level at the resorts below, from the coldest to the warmest hour of each day: Sunday 8 November: from 8,530 ft (2,600 m) to 14,436 ft (4,400 m). Monday 9 November: from 8,202 ft (2,500 m) to 14,370 ft (4,380 m). Tuesday 10 November: from 7,513 ft (2,290 m) to 14,173 ft (4,320 m). Wednesday 11 November: from 7,349 ft (2,240 m) to 13,845 ft (4,220 m)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "How snow turns to rain below the 0 °C level",
     "p": [
      "Falling snow meets warmer air below the 0 °C level and starts to melt, but it needs time and energy to turn into rain, so the change happens some 984 ft (300 m) lower: that is the snow line. Evaporation in dry air cools the flakes and can lower it further."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "advice": [
    "Warm air softens the snow by midday, so ski high and on north-facing slopes early.",
    "A forecast 0 °C level can be off by several hundred metres; check the map again the day before you go.",
    "Before any off-piste or touring day, read the official avalanche bulletin (links below).",
    "Snow on the map does not mean a resort is open: check lifts and dates on the resort's website."
   ],
   "captions": {
